"\n\n<p>The previous essay ended with a hint. This construct had begun to run under Washington, acknowledging partisan strife as a remainder it would inevitably generate and could only accommodate, refusing to pursue a unified will without disagreement. But acknowledging a remainder does not mean the remainder will always be treated gently. John Adams's four years were the first time this construct encountered truly systemic pressure — and its first response to that pressure was to try to expel the very remainder it was supposed to contain.</p>\n\n<p>Adams took office in an awkward position. He was the first successor to Washington, carrying the inherent burden of comparison with the founder. More troubling still, the Federalist Party did not genuinely cohere around him — Hamilton held the party's effective influence. Modern research describes Adams's situation bluntly: he was politically isolated, his own cabinet often opposed him. By autumn 1799, his unilateral push for a second peace mission to France had openly fractured his relationship with his department heads. By the election of 1800, Hamilton had published a pamphlet openly attacking the sitting president of his own party.</p>\n\n<p>This essay's starting point is therefore not a verdict on whether Adams was a misunderstood good president. It is a structural observation: the first pressure test of this construct examined not any president's character but whether the construct itself could keep functioning under the combined squeeze of external threat and internal party warfare. A freshly learning institution, encountering its first simultaneous real external threat and fierce internal strife, faced exactly the temptation any ruling group would face — to eliminate the source of the trouble, meaning dissent itself. Its first answer was a closing impulse.</p>\n\n<h3>1. A Real Crisis</h3>\n\n<p>To describe this closing impulse accurately, one must first acknowledge that it was not manufactured hysteria — it was built on a real crisis. France had been deeply angered by the Jay Treaty, viewing it as American alignment with Britain, and from 1796 onward had expanded seizures of American merchant vessels. The Directory, under financial strain from European war and furious at Anglo-American rapprochement, allowed French privateers to prey on American shipping. More than three hundred American ships were seized. Adams faced a genuine diplomatic and maritime security crisis.</p>\n\n<p>The XYZ Affair was the emotional turning point. In 1797, Adams sent Pinckney, Marshall, and Gerry to Paris to negotiate a repair of relations. The envoys arrived to find that Talleyrand's agents — referred to as X, Y, and Z in the published documents — demanded a loan and personal payments as preconditions for any talks. When Adams submitted the documents to Congress, public sentiment swung sharply anti-French. Adams reached the peak of his public approval rating. He signed legislation creating the Navy Department on April 30, 1798, and what followed was a Quasi-War — an undeclared naval conflict in the Caribbean and Atlantic rather than a formally declared full war. The Quasi-War had dual institutional effects: it helped the young federal government demonstrate its capacity to protect shipping and maintain diplomatic leverage, while simultaneously providing the justification for treating domestic political dissent as a security threat.</p>\n\n<p>The structural pattern this episode exemplifies will recur throughout this series. The closing impulse does not typically arise from pure malice. It follows a fairly fixed path: first a real external crisis generates real security anxiety; then that anxiety is internalized into domestic partisan combat; then one side of the partisan fight re-encodes it as law, quietly overlapping the external enemy with domestic political opponents, so that vigilance against foreign threats slides into punishment of internal dissent. The XYZ Affair and Quasi-War supplied genuine anxiety; the Sedition Act converted that anxiety into criminal law. Understanding this path matters because it shows the closing impulse does not require a villain — only a real crisis and the temptation to treat political opponents as dangers.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Expelling Dissent from the Institution</h3>\n\n<p>The four laws of 1798 are often remembered as a single great suppression package, but their legal structures differ. The first extended naturalization from five to fourteen years, raising the threshold for new immigrants to become voters. The second gave the president peacetime power to expel aliens deemed dangerous. The third authorized detention and deportation of alien enemies during declared war or invasion threat, and had no sunset clause. The fourth — the Sedition Act — was the one that struck at the institutional core by bringing domestic political criticism into federal criminal law.</p>\n\n<p>The Sedition Act's text is worth examining closely. Its first section penalized unlawful combination or conspiracy to oppose government measures, impede federal law, intimidate federal officials, or instigate riot or illegal assembly, with penalties up to five thousand dollars and five years in prison. The second section — the part that lives in constitutional memory — penalized writing, printing, or publishing any false, scandalous, or malicious text against the federal government, either house of Congress, or the president, with intent to bring them into contempt, excite hatred, or stir up opposition to law — punishable by up to two thousand dollars and two years in prison. A third section nominally allowed truth as a defense and allowed juries to judge law as well as fact. A fourth section set the law to expire on March 3, 1801.</p>\n\n<p>Two details illuminate the nature of this closing impulse. First, the law's protection covered the federal government, either house of Congress, and the president — but conspicuously not the vice president, who was Jefferson. In the heat of partisan conflict, attacking the ruling party's president was criminalized; attacking the opposition leader was not. Second, while the truth defense and jury power sounded fair in principle, the practical space they created was very narrow. Political criticism consists primarily of judgment and satire, not verifiable factual claims. Saying a president's conduct is absurd, or that a policy is dangerous, are opinions that cannot be proved true in a courtroom. The nominal defense thus offered little real protection for most political speech.</p>\n\n<p>The structural meaning is clear. This was the construct's first closing impulse. The previous essay established that what distinguishes this construct from an order pursuing closure is its acknowledgment of partisan strife as legitimate, giving it the channels of separation of powers, press, and elections. The Sedition Act attempted the reverse: to use federal criminal law to expel the remainder from inside the institution, redefining political opponents from objects to be accommodated into criminals to be punished. It was the construct's first attempt to eliminate rather than contain its own generated remainder.</p>\n\n<h3>3. How It Was Enforced, and Why Dissent Survived</h3>\n\n<p>The old account held that roughly fifteen Republicans were prosecuted under the Sedition Act between 1798 and 1801, with about ten going to trial. Recent archival research by historian Wendell Bird has substantially revised this figure, identifying up to fifty-one prosecutions with thirty-three defendants — suggesting the suppression was broader than the compressed memory later preserved. The revision matters not merely for accuracy but because it shows the 1798 federal suppression was not a handful of symbolic cases; it was a genuinely punitive enforcement moment whose full scope was later condensed in historical memory.</p>\n\n<p>Matthew Lyon of Vermont was the most emblematic case. He was a sitting congressman, prosecuted in October 1798 for scathing published criticism of Adams's conduct in office. He was convicted and sentenced to four months in prison and a thousand-dollar fine. What made the case remarkable was that Lyon won re-election from jail. An incumbent congressman, criminalized by the ruling party, was returned to Congress by his constituents. The legal case became a political case about whether an opposition could survive inside the institution at all — and voters answered by overruling the verdict at the ballot box. This is exactly the awkwardness that a closing impulse encounters inside a construct that acknowledges remainders: you can imprison a person, but you cannot simultaneously cancel the channel that lets him return to the institution, unless you abolish that channel entirely — and abolishing it means abolishing the construct itself.</p>\n\n<p>Although the Supreme Court never formally adjudicated the Sedition Act during its lifetime, it became a negative precedent in American constitutional memory. A century and a half later, the Court in a landmark free speech decision stated that the attack on the Act's validity had been carried in the court of history. This is important for understanding how this construct corrects its errors — not always by getting things right at the time, but by gradually forming a constitutional memory that says this must not happen again.</p>\n\n<h3>4. The Virginia and Kentucky Resolutions: Dissent Transferred</h3>\n\n<p>The first systematic counterattack on the closing impulse came from the state level — and it introduced a long-running problem into American political language. Jefferson and Madison secretly drafted the Virginia and Kentucky Resolutions, which were passed by their respective state legislatures. Their immediate purpose was to declare the Alien and Sedition Acts unconstitutional. Their argumentative method was to characterize the Constitution as a compact among states and to assert that when the federal government overstepped, the states as parties to the compact had the right to judge and resist.</p>\n\n<p>The two documents differed in wording. Virginia's resolution spoke of interposition. Kentucky's original draft, in Jefferson's hand, used the word nullification — asserting that states faced with unauthorized federal acts could declare those acts void within their borders. But the word nullification was removed from the final text Kentucky actually passed. The distinction matters: Jefferson's draft and the enacted resolution were not identical, and the history of influence runs through both versions.</p>\n\n<p>No other states joined the protest. Of the fourteen that responded, four gave no answer and ten explicitly rejected the resolutions' reasoning. They did not succeed in reversing the federal legislation. But they introduced into American political vocabulary the option of the state as ultimate interpreter — a dangerous option that would be taken up by very different forces in very different circumstances decades later. The most stable historical assessment runs on two tracks simultaneously: these texts did provide language resources that later nullification and even secessionist arguments would cite, while equating Madison and Jefferson's 1798 position directly with antebellum nullification theory would be a serious overreach. The texts were a vocabulary library; how that vocabulary was later used exceeded their authors' intent.</p>\n\n<p>The structural point is clear: the closing impulse tried to expel dissent from the institution. Dissent was not eliminated; it moved — from the federal level to the question of federal-state relations — and continued to exist and contend. A remainder acknowledged by a construct can evolve, change form, and still remain within the institution's field of vision.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Adams's Peace</h3>\n\n<p>Beyond the closing impulse and the state-level counterattack, the most underappreciated feature of Adams's presidency is the second peace mission to France in 1799. On February 18, Adams informed the Senate that he was nominating William Vans Murray as minister to France. Within a week he expanded the mission. This was politically enormous: with the Quasi-War ongoing, war sentiment still strong, and the Federalist leadership fracturing, Adams reopened the diplomatic exit. The cost was his political isolation. His unilateral decision deepened his rift with his cabinet, and his loss in the election of 1800 owed substantially to being abandoned by his own party.</p>\n\n<p>The Convention of 1800 ended the Quasi-War and annulled the 1778 Treaty of Alliance with France — the only formal alliance the United States had ever ratified. Adams later wrote that he wished for no other inscription on his gravestone than that he had taken responsibility for peace with France in 1800. This judgment should neither be used to erase the Sedition Act from his record, nor should the Sedition Act be used to erase this judgment. The same man signed a law criminalizing dissent and pulled the country back from the edge of full-scale war against his own party's wishes. Both are true, and structural analysis holds them together rather than choosing between them.</p>\n\n<h3>6. The Election of 1800 and the Transfer of 1801</h3>\n\n<p>The election of 1800 matters less because of how vicious it was — and it was spectacularly vicious, with Hamilton openly attacking his own party's president and Federalists depicting Jefferson as godless and dangerous — than because it was the first time the question of peaceful transfer of power between hostile parties became a real test rather than a theoretical aspiration.</p>\n\n<p>Jefferson and Aaron Burr tied at seventy-three electoral votes each. Under the original constitutional rules, the choice went to the outgoing Federalist-controlled House of Representatives, which balloted thirty-five times over five days before selecting Jefferson on the thirty-sixth ballot on February 17, 1801. The deadlock exposed that the original electoral system had been designed without anticipating political parties — it had not foreseen that presidential and vice-presidential candidates would be nominated on a single party ticket.</p>\n\n<p>The Twelfth Amendment, ratified in 1804, was the direct institutional response: electors would henceforth cast separate ballots for president and vice president. The significance for this construct is fundamental. The crisis of 1800 was not resolved by declaring that there should be no more parties. It was resolved by rewriting the rules to acknowledge that parties already existed. This construct's response to a remainder it generated was not to eliminate the remainder but to adjust its own rules to accommodate it. It did not pretend parties did not exist; it rewrote its rules to accept that they did.</p>\n\n<p>The peaceful transfer of March 1801 was therefore symbolically decisive. It was the first time the United States transferred power from one party to its direct opponent without violence. This happened after federal criminal law had suppressed dissent, state legislatures had publicly denounced federal overreach, party coalitions had openly split, and the election had produced a constitutional deadlock. That the transfer still occurred under all these conditions was a powerful proof of what an open-ended construct can endure.</p>\n\n<p>The transfer was peaceful but not clean. Before leaving, the Federalist Congress passed the Judiciary Act of 1801, reducing Supreme Court seats from six to five (to prevent Jefferson from making an immediate appointment), reorganizing the circuit court system, and creating sixteen new circuit judge positions. Republicans derided these as midnight appointments — a final attempt by a defeated party to embed its influence in the judiciary after losing the elected branches. The midnight judges would generate one of the defining cases of the next presidency. The peaceful transfer, then, was peaceful and full of maneuvering at the same time — which is precisely what a construct that channels conflict into rules rather than streets looks like at its most honest.</p>\n\n<h3>7. Conclusion</h3>\n\n<p>Adams's four years provide a complete template of what a closing impulse looks like inside an open-ended construct, and a complete record of this construct's first systemic pressure test.</p>\n\n<p>The Sedition Act and the election and transfer of 1800–1801 must be read together, not separately. The former was the closing impulse; the latter was how the impulse was reabsorbed. Reabsorption happened on several levels simultaneously: the Act's built-in sunset clause let it expire automatically on March 3, 1801; state resistance relocated dissent to the federal-state relationship; elections and the transfer channeled fierce partisan competition back inside the rules; and a constitutional memory gradually formed that this must not happen again. The construct did not correct itself through a single decisive moment; it corrected itself through this accumulation of absorptions at different levels.</p>\n\n<p>Two threads ran forward from this pressure test. One was the state-as-ultimate-interpreter language, which had served here to resist a genuine federal overreach, but which also deposited a dangerous vocabulary that would be drawn on for very different purposes much later. The other was the question of executive power versus judicial independence, which the midnight judges had pushed to the foreground. The incoming Jefferson, who had come to office on a platform of strict constitutional construction and limited federal power, would inherit rather than dismantle the state machine his opponents had built — and would face at the outset the puzzle the midnight judges had created. That paradox is what the next essay is about.</p>\n\n      "
